erred to as hyper networks  uh sometimes and it's possible that this  will not retain some of the induct nice  inductive biases of neural networks when  the weights come from another neural  network  um  and we will actually see in the kind of  coming lectures approaches that have  better inductive biases including metric  learning approaches uh and a more  optimization-based approaches  um where the weights aren't coming from  another Network  yeah so what is how many tasks do we  need in general like I said before the  more the better  um and it really it ends up being  somewhat problem specific and domain  specific if the tasks are relatively  simple you might not need that many  um if you have a very broad distribution  of tasks and you're going to need more  tasks to cover that broad distribution  there is some work that tries to  theoretically characterize how the  number of tasks impacts performance I  think that there's some work by Richard  zimmel for example at Toronto that  studies that and they do look at some  bounds but that's uh kind of beyond what  we'll look at in this course  okay um and then a couple more things  worth mentioning here um this was an  example in Omniglot there are also a  number of other image recognition data  sets like cfar Cubs celebrate and orbit  Orbit's actually a pretty nice example  it's it's coming from the standpoint of  trying to build classifiers for blind or  visually impaired users where the user  will like take pictures of their items  I'll take only a few pictures naturally  and then you want to be able to allow  them to build a classifier for their  items using those pictures  um there's also benchmarks that are  outside of the image recognition domain  here are a few examples and if you're  interested in looking at data sets for  your projects these benchmarks could be  interesting  okay  um and then to summarize uh kind of the  Black Box approach and this pros and  cons  um these sorts of networks are very  expressive because they're essentially  parameterizing a learning procedure  through using a potentially very large  neural network  um it's also very easy to combine this  kind of approach with a variety of  different problems including supervised  learning and reinforcement learning  um there are some downsides so in  general you're trying to learn how to  learn from scratch essentially you're  trying to learn this learning procedure  from completely randomly initialize the  weights of a neural network and this  leads to a fairly challenging  optimization procedure  and as a result it can sometimes be data  inefficient in terms of the tasks that  you need for meta training  and so there are other ways that we can  essentially represent these learning  procedures and next time on Wednesday  we're going to be looking at how we can  essentially treat it as an optimization  procedure and parametrize an  optimization procedure  um  as part of the learning process rather  than using a neural network to represent  the entire learning procedure
 

